#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:1
msgid "Content-Addressing Store Objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:3
msgid ""
"Just [like](../file-system-object/content-address.md) [File System Objects]"
"(../file-system-object.md), [Store Objects](../store-object.md) can also be "
"[content-addressed](../../glossary.md#gloss-content-address), unless they "
"are [input-addressed](../../glossary.md#gloss-input-addressed-store-object)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:7
msgid ""
"For store objects, the content address we produce will take the form of a "
"[Store Path](../store-path.md) rather than regular hash. In particular, the "
"content-addressing scheme will ensure that the digest of the store path is "
"solely computed from the"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:10
msgid "file system object graph (the root one and its children, if it has any)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:11
msgid "references"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:12
msgid "[store directory path](../store-path.md#store-directory-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:13
msgid "name"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:15
msgid ""
"of the store object, and not any other information, which would not be an "
"intrinsic property of that store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:17
msgid ""
"For the full specification of the algorithms involved, see the "
"[specification of store path digests](../../protocols/store-path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:23
msgid "Content addressing each part of a store object"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:25
msgid "File System Objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:27
msgid ""
"With all currently-supported store object content-addressing methods, the "
"file system object is always [content-addressed](../file-system-object/"
"content-address.md) first, and then that hash is incorporated into content "
"address computation for the store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:29
msgid "References"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:31
msgid "References to other store objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:33
msgid ""
"With all currently supported store object content addressing methods, other "
"objects are referred to by their regular (string-encoded-) [store paths](../"
"store-path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:36
msgid "Self-references"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:38
msgid ""
"Self-references however cannot be referred to by their path, because we are "
"in the midst of describing how to compute that path!"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:40
msgid ""
"The alternative would require finding as hash function fixed point, i.e. the "
"solution to an equation in the form"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:44
msgid ""
"which is computationally infeasible. As far as we know, this is equivalent "
"to finding a hash collision."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:47
msgid ""
"Instead we have a \"has self-reference\" boolean, which ends up affecting "
"the digest: In all currently-supported store object content-addressing "
"methods, when hashing the file system object data, any occurrence of store "
"object's own store path in the digested data is replaced with a [sentinel "
"value](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentinel_value). The hashes of these "
"modified input streams are used instead."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:51
msgid ""
"When validating the content address of a store object after the fact, the "
"above process works as written. However, when first creating the store "
"object we don't know the store object's store path, as explained just above. "
"We therefore, strictly speaking, do not know what value we will be replacing "
"with the sentinel value in the inputs to hash functions. What instead "
"happens is that the provisional store object --- the data from which we wish "
"to create a store object --- is paired with a provisional \"scratch\" store "
"path (that presumably was chosen when the data was created). That "
"provisional store path is instead what is replaced with the sentinel value, "
"rather than the final store object which we do not yet know."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:57
msgid "**Design note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:59
msgid ""
"It is an informal property of content-addressed store objects that the "
"choice of provisional store path should not matter. In other words, if a "
"provisional store object is prepared in the same way except for the choice "
"of provision store path, the provisional data need not be identical. But, "
"after the sentinel value is substituted in place of each provisional store "
"object's provision store path, the final so-normalized data _should_ be "
"identical."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:63
msgid ""
"If, conversely, the data after this normalization process is still "
"different, we'll compute a different content-address. The method of "
"preparing the provisional self-referenced data has _failed_ to be "
"deterministic in the sense of not _leaking_ the choice of provisional store "
"path --- a choice which is supposed to be arbitrary --- into the final store "
"object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:66
msgid ""
"This property is informal at this stage, because we are just describing "
"store objects, which have no formal notion of their origin. Without that, we "
"cannot rigorously define what it would mean for the process to be "
"insufficiently deterministic. When we cover [derivations](../../store/"
"derivation/index.md), we will be able to make this a formal property — not "
"of content-addressed store objects themselves, but of the derivations that "
"_produce_ them."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:70
msgid "Name and Store Directory"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:72
msgid ""
"These two items affect the digest in a way that is standard for store path "
"digest computations and not specific to content-addressing. Consult the "
"[specification of store path digests](../../protocols/store-path.md) for "
"further details."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:75
msgid "Content addressing Methods"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:77
msgid ""
"For historical reasons, we don't support all features in all combinations. "
"Each currently supported method of content addressing chooses a single "
"method of file system object hashing, and may offer some restrictions on "
"references. The names and store directories are unrestricted however."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:81
msgid "Flat"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:83
msgid ""
"This uses the corresponding [Flat](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-flat) method of file system object content addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:85
msgid ""
"References are not supported: store objects with flat hashing _and_ "
"references can not be created."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:87
msgid "Text"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:89
msgid ""
"This also uses the corresponding [Flat](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-flat) method of file system object content addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:91
msgid ""
"References to other store objects are supported, but self-references are not."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:93
msgid ""
"This is the only store-object content-addressing method that is not named "
"identically with a corresponding file system object method. It is somewhat "
"obscure, mainly used for \"drv files\" (derivations serialized as store "
"objects in their [\"ATerm\" file format](../../protocols/derivation-"
"aterm.md#use-for-encoding-to-store-object)). Prefer another method if "
"possible."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:98
msgid "Nix Archive"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:100
msgid ""
"This uses the corresponding [Nix Archive](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-nix-archive) method of file system object content "
"addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:102
msgid ""
"References (to other store objects and self-references alike) are supported "
"so long as the hash algorithm is SHA-256, but not (neither kind) otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:104
msgid "Git"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:106
msgid "**Warning**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:108
msgid ""
"This method is part of the [`git-hashing`](../../development/experimental-"
"features.md#xp-feature-git-hashing) experimental feature."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:110
msgid ""
"This uses the corresponding [Git](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#git) method of file system object content addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:112
msgid "References are not supported."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:114
msgid ""
"Only SHA-1 is supported at this time. If [SHA-256-based Git](https://git-"
"scm.com/docs/hash-function-transition) becomes more widespread, this "
"restriction will be revisited."
msgstr ""
